#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119659 :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\n\t\t\\left\\{ \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\t\\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim} \\left( \\textrm{Span}\n\t\t\\left\\{ \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\t\\right\\} \\right) = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119660 :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\n\t\t\\left\\{ \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 6 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ - 15 \\\\ 10\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\t\\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim} \\left( \\textrm{Span}\n\t\t\\left\\{ \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 6 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ - 15 \\\\ 10\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\t\\right\\} \\right) = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119687 :question/id #uuid "85acf106-2f03-5509-8ad0-08b82ef2a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8005" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\,\n{\\left| \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & - 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & - 4 & - 2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & - 2 & - 3 & 3 \\\\ - 3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{array} \\right|}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(11-3) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119690 :question/id #uuid "4a9954b2-3d4b-5669-88cd-6e74c9683a60" :question/math-academy-id "e-8054" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119691 :question/id #uuid "ccf558b9-bf00-58c7-b244-2c5797f93d8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8060" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841b51ab-4624-5135-9e62-a5d16f7f40d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118305} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5c3ff9-d114-5657-833d-571463f53ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118306} {:answer/id #uuid "0095d96f-0e33-50d5-bec2-0593e0bb2a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118307} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd230b8-acb7-514d-8c02-14cbb3854aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} {:answer/id #uuid "43fd4f00-8337-5638-b2ff-f732db2e10d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe09fbe-4ea8-5884-b7ed-064efcb293bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3ea0ae2-1531-5d26-b98b-bdd05134f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34811" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0779cf5d-0317-57a8-976b-e5c32094a673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317596} {:answer/id #uuid "099edd94-bc66-57a1-ad68-2beab5cdb78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317601} {:answer/id #uuid "0c07126e-1c42-58af-8ff4-69376bbbdc11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317609} {:answer/id #uuid "5a75dfe7-7239-5b56-94b2-b06b2cdeb992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317870} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0b55af-6c16-50b6-91ef-aa8e1e1a89fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318094}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318094} :answer-field/id #uuid "495781e1-1d03-5210-8f3a-8afc23850a24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5704a84d-0989-5302-98de-8c50b889e0c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34854" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a9045a-7bce-5521-9e5e-c18cda1bc188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118629} {:answer/id #uuid "1428c966-b14f-5658-a6f5-11220376879d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118630} {:answer/id #uuid "2370d1bf-4d63-5ef2-82b0-1bf300a84b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118631} {:answer/id #uuid "47b9c8a5-e5a1-5b17-a0a2-47a2f155c5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118632} {:answer/id #uuid "5f193840-cca0-5ffb-95a1-ae4e4782955b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118632} :answer-field/id #uuid "67dfd6a1-fb8e-5c81-a959-a13da9e1b424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "150c328e-7322-5fa3-b605-dd458b140db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-42075" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{11}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4-(-2)) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b47765-953e-5c0d-a6e0-6fc049b9d66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326994} {:answer/id #uuid "5f24b764-fc2b-5e7d-bd75-a8a89294f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327770} {:answer/id #uuid "782d2a8e-c0e2-5d32-8bc0-5016b689fde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-504" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327961} {:answer/id #uuid "9492a067-189c-5486-8f3f-228fbe940631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328232} {:answer/id #uuid "fae37e69-e3d8-55ce-838e-b4d8a157524f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328232} :answer-field/id #uuid "61379f67-edb6-5505-bc07-8f1b8c83ae04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37774ae0-a0cd-5a39-9730-2063497da256" :question/math-academy-id "q-42559" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -8 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -8 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-3){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-2){C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -5 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -5 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(-28+40) \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b962086-642e-5457-b307-77aeda149c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -7 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -11 & -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118719} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e22c3b-c3e3-5b3c-8e10-d933ecf3a117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -7 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118720} {:answer/id #uuid "b43b18ef-645a-57f3-9cbd-9fe464290792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -11\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118721} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c35ff9-a44b-5069-9c7b-d5f6ddcad57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -7 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -11 & -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118722} {:answer/id #uuid "20baa029-88dd-5bd7-8ee4-4702c736af29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118723}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3a8690ad-dd1e-5a2f-95da-6cc6f46593c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118718}] :question/id #uuid "12705466-6c80-5c6f-bd52-24095c209938" :question/math-academy-id "q-42574" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4 \\cdot 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3 \\cdot 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above $a_{42}$ equal to zero.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fcef5e-b3e2-5ccd-ad74-3f0f2bf5215e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327159} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef0a0f8-a3e1-5867-a44d-53d8501860b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328178} {:answer/id #uuid "9b76b2b0-7b79-5bed-b323-bb17d564fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328279} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b548cb-6708-57bd-8aac-098ad056ea48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328668} {:answer/id #uuid "e9942f7d-5789-519b-8b26-e8f47afefdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328982}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327159} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c7dcea-54be-5b44-9e93-100efe093b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b66a6d15-0b61-5b61-a152-b57545d60ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42581" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{vmatrix}=-7,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{vmatrix}}_{-7}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} {:answer/id #uuid "104af8d6-6df5-59fb-8200-c18490b4fd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327079} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8e5b8a-f047-5394-af88-102417a6f0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327338} {:answer/id #uuid "3fde6c7e-e80c-5ec0-964a-81bf5acc2144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327492} {:answer/id #uuid "c150ce04-23ed-5152-a4c5-802328f796f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} :answer-field/id #uuid "7775f4a0-2beb-5ee4-8f08-e48b58e45535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e713fddd-f608-51f9-ac5a-f16a9c918c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-42584" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(12-12)+(-15+24)+2(10-16) \\\\  & =0+9-12 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884c1925-0ccc-54e5-8235-15f4852ab5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} {:answer/id #uuid "974ae98b-f65f-5d56-abb2-235fcf5d1709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118810} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f4f7fc-49f1-51bb-a52f-e78439376e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118811} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c1329a-446a-56c8-94dc-f3d8fb69e271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118812} {:answer/id #uuid "665f03b3-8fe1-5194-816a-e67e96a71c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} :answer-field/id #uuid "56464aea-61e2-5f04-a701-b6c9323c4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "daa9792e-2290-5f44-bdfd-5cde9ef8123b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42996" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802710b7-06ce-5984-836b-24d23e908795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118815} {:answer/id #uuid "88d08bdb-91b6-5612-8866-6c32d2154455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118816} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd3681-8559-584e-8dc4-8e32d28a8f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118817} {:answer/id #uuid "24ae822d-ae24-5f9e-bd9e-7163297a8bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118818} {:answer/id #uuid "4e032d56-fe86-578f-8a93-563fa1d41804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118819}] :answer-field/id #uuid "26e0f963-8adf-5d0a-9939-805f848f51b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118814}] :question/id #uuid "74f90825-c881-5b88-bb77-5380cede5022" :question/math-academy-id "q-43062" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa174c3-be74-5a68-a41e-293625f948d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327165} {:answer/id #uuid "45a4f0b7-ab1c-507f-a3c0-51640e4f16be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327543} {:answer/id #uuid "5200235b-4b2e-514a-8db5-4aa6adadf377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327648} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f8baab-a97f-50d5-965a-f8b1d544e5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328357} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8e009-9985-5a9f-88ca-075fd371139e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329000} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c99cfa-c616-5ee5-9ec1-28b017f9b14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f613c1e6-df74-5921-9adf-aac6f2ef23de" :question/math-academy-id "q-43063" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =12+3 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e097d8-e542-5acf-a330-fe6eeb6f27a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326952} {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} {:answer/id #uuid "a0898f35-ae35-5a2c-85c3-2c1973fb0a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "870" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328324} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d333b3-316e-527a-9482-d8e7753af3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b3cbac-6be7-58e9-bfd2-408356f2bda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a480665-7d5c-59ec-8577-d65fbb82b058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6ac6b8ae-6cc0-5802-bd63-e9b3982c4a3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43097" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa8197c-4d82-5088-9b64-1ef6e345a537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119289} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa93a08-9f46-569e-ae1a-706babb3838d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119290} {:answer/id #uuid "da46756d-4478-5fed-a930-ff9293835334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119291} {:answer/id #uuid "193c650a-2471-5fc1-a082-f49a3c75fb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119292} {:answer/id #uuid "53b26439-37ef-554d-b6f5-c8b08105b18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119293} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0b9265-9f6c-5308-ba6e-2f626ee63728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "487f0ee8-db6c-59bd-83cb-1d43b5a535da" :question/math-academy-id "q-79143" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9d9b911-3c3f-58c5-b3b6-d44535560355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119295} {:answer/id #uuid "df4d4fcd-8faf-530e-b415-5c1f3fed2066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119296} {:answer/id #uuid "e52dbb26-c23e-57e3-b4cf-35ee9f0f3f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119297} {:answer/id #uuid "0de4b9c7-15f2-56b8-8c4e-d86894ba45f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119298} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb91a0b-9c2a-5f24-90dc-175b0eb4a659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119295} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac61baf-9210-59b4-8248-21725e9864ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b165675-1f3d-55a7-a1a0-5f0b3adccb41" :question/math-academy-id "q-79149" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=4$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ (not $4$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fec93138-cf0b-5d3f-8a5c-8882046ae062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394835} {:answer/id #uuid "090d23f3-9df7-5a18-ad1c-3041ca3b0b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394836} {:answer/id #uuid "c4475098-4c19-544f-a8c8-75b11b40afce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394837} {:answer/id #uuid "0720eaf4-1dba-52f4-98df-0fc8cc7f0d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394838} {:answer/id #uuid "c47f2fc0-2674-5e12-89b3-daeb57faaf22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394835} :answer-field/id #uuid "294d6d11-4a04-5aed-b0e6-84671260549e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ea53fce-52af-53d1-b17c-6cd66b5ff019" :question/math-academy-id "q-91092" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}=2$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-1){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06aaaf7d-04b8-5a23-9953-cd9e101e8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354713} {:answer/id #uuid "082e25b1-1db4-55d6-958d-e024808599d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354735} {:answer/id #uuid "662ded31-c64a-5e2b-9c69-28ae5100fa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355574} {:answer/id #uuid "763a2e4b-6fe0-5469-945f-945f29b1e998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355700} {:answer/id #uuid "a30dd06e-3ed0-5694-a5bc-cc70e6ce490b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355574} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b944774-455d-57fc-bd5c-40c834715311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8b50beb4-268d-5e94-a279-936396d00949" :question/math-academy-id "q-91166" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below ${a}_{14}=3$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -11 & 4 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -11 & 4 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46cd8000-1e23-5199-b2db-301eb99e864d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394862} {:answer/id #uuid "1289a79f-f03a-57c5-80ce-7fd42973e8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394863} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2989e5-bdd3-537a-85ba-5abd1555a897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394864} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd0f37a-c44f-5c54-9e1a-bd783a206783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394865} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ae3856-08c7-541a-9fe1-12f594788fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8b159e-b89f-5a64-b5d5-e5aa63c40762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2a2b160-a569-5696-8596-179bbcebb9ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-91220" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}=2$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-3){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-1){C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184063 :question/id #uuid "61a0b8a7-332d-5ae1-ad46-a65369bc87d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4415" :question/problem "Find $B^2$ given that \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Computing $B^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $B^2=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184064 :question/id #uuid "906ff9b7-c304-5b9a-b152-cef3bf343de1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4416" :question/problem "Find $C^5$ given that\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute $C^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $C^2=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute $C^5$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{5} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n\n\n$$\nC^5=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184070 :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184071 :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\left. \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\;\\right| \\;x_1, x_3 \\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}?\n$$\n\n \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\;\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} }\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184124 :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60befaec-36ec-5bcc-9008-ed35714da6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390732} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e1cbf6-bcee-5647-9c18-035106ecccb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390733} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bc671c-e1db-57b1-898c-d81ac0904685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390734} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca15efe-dcb6-5128-9996-8b2ce0db2597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390735} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bcb91d-1c62-5830-889e-d2d622d846ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390734} :answer-field/id #uuid "b467dda5-da42-5b7b-b028-d10f4cde0503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dafad545-0385-51d6-9233-37b97e1f8f85" :question/math-academy-id "q-32323" :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {B}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961371f2-f71a-573d-9b98-504222de4a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183134} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bdf09-8484-50e4-907e-eeceb6bf01c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183135} {:answer/id #uuid "a9063a2f-983f-511b-8320-eb5479f9dbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183136} {:answer/id #uuid "37b465af-8d04-53f5-a8eb-c31f56a99ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183137} {:answer/id #uuid "781325c3-e9c6-5691-9247-c29a5c79d34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183136} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef2d522-f80e-5da1-ba53-1027339ce406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a05d1874-f484-5b03-a7ba-b2697b84a74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34833" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=3,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17adadd-02fe-5b13-a556-c4b68557dec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183140} {:answer/id #uuid "f44f8717-6bc7-5c29-a85d-01286768fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183141} {:answer/id #uuid "3493b4eb-bc91-59c3-abe3-1362d436c2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183142} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d43af-17a2-5f2e-916b-b2453eace3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183143} {:answer/id #uuid "62186aa1-6d7f-5a56-8e00-28c657c126cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183141} :answer-field/id #uuid "405282dd-7ce6-56bf-8492-d4b7cbb14f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4d185801-460b-5883-a896-8f1107477aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34930" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}?\n$$\n\nI. The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the generating vectors in \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nare linearly independent, we conclude that the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4.$ Now, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5bc6ca8-9ac0-5308-a6b2-87c27295178c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183146} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5cc6e5-5ce0-553b-97f4-aa903d5a2873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183147} {:answer/id #uuid "06d1858b-b3ba-5fd6-be5b-352c28e50337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183148} {:answer/id #uuid "351bf408-a43f-531a-94a0-4268e98ac1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183149} {:answer/id #uuid "77c26f94-f1e0-5b92-90d4-af981ae4d341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183147} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce549fcc-13ed-50dd-8870-5c2475c33016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "245a8171-aeb5-5caf-950d-44cbb3c0aece" :question/math-academy-id "q-34931" :question/problem "Consider a $6\\times 7$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c103f-607d-5f86-b626-7f905a9009be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183152} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5566c6-f36e-5fa6-963f-ff29a059239e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183153} {:answer/id #uuid "1eee22c0-aaee-5c76-b935-89d9e34d716f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183154} {:answer/id #uuid "21dd967c-ba0d-5081-815f-e4707b87b16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183155} {:answer/id #uuid "6a680598-5a75-5f51-afed-93c0cd66c558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183153} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c0659d5-6754-5a2c-b59d-a8d4e248b40e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50dd3dbf-46fe-5a60-971b-ec20882fc4a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-34934" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2c253b-eb4a-53cb-b0ca-7c84c3094ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183164} {:answer/id #uuid "a1905378-ce91-51d4-9b0f-bc5b3501e65e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183165} {:answer/id #uuid "3829fa6b-44cd-591e-a98f-f251876ca514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183166} {:answer/id #uuid "44fd0839-e352-55e5-a108-928603beaa61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183167} {:answer/id #uuid "499609e4-1cbc-5b03-a3fc-4d0860e6c9f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183168} :answer-field/id #uuid "229b5487-34b1-598b-9b5d-3ffee9c57d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67b28820-a085-5f18-884d-33b6d40e4f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-35306" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abafdde9-cae5-582c-8e9f-4abfcfbcb322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183170} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d831b4-ce0f-5ab0-a85d-095692b8ec9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183171} {:answer/id #uuid "e3aec90e-a80b-5dc9-8aa0-32459cfe2ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183172} {:answer/id #uuid "ed949a79-7417-534c-9f56-fa03f1c6bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183173} {:answer/id #uuid "15b05d56-f68c-51c4-80b9-8c5988ed2e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183173} :answer-field/id #uuid "45888489-94ed-541f-8e81-9c58b0bb07c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a160f7a5-3a4d-5809-8834-75195b7301e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-35433" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 6$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b338931-0010-5e28-82c4-3aefc60dee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183200} {:answer/id #uuid "c45410be-e36d-5e8b-8ffb-aa69736f69fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-322 & -131 \\\\ 786 & -453\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183201} {:answer/id #uuid "dcda3de3-35ca-5552-8a92-1743cde4ba28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183202} {:answer/id #uuid "157da582-e8f3-53ae-898c-45d80e92bab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-62 & 181 \\\\ 1086 & 843\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183203} {:answer/id #uuid "44b24393-ed19-5f7e-ad90-61b057129664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183204}] :answer-field/id #uuid "67f4409c-ff33-55cb-9de9-cc75bb8b668d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05542273-c068-59e6-abed-a5b60fb013e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36304" :question/problem "If $B = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then $B^{5} =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f00329-8339-5c8f-955e-93d3cc47132c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 27 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321720} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9f9363-dac4-573d-a23e-59671a908126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 18 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322270} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5f5b78-6857-53be-a2ff-bb42b9d3d3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322616} {:answer/id #uuid "f97c696f-c007-5401-bea2-af96124c497a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323108} {:answer/id #uuid "fea87382-29c0-5f10-843d-a1372d5d1b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322616} :answer-field/id #uuid "86599986-00bf-5188-9938-26770828abc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "122c0975-4dfe-5f37-a6e1-cdeef5051652" :question/math-academy-id "q-36315" :question/problem "If $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${E}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${E}^{2}=E,$ so $E$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${E}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{4} & ={E}^{2}\\cdot {E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & ={E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{E}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]}
